          "explanation": "Flu mutates its surface proteins, mainly hemagglutinin, a little each season -- antigenic drift -- so last year's antibodies fit less well and vaccines are updated yearly. Measles keeps essentially the same appearance, so memory from vaccination or infection stays a good match for life. The tempting 'more antigen' answer misses the point: the issue is a moving target, not dose. Flu vaccines do generate B and T cell memory.",
