          "explanation": "A CRP above 10 mg/L usually reflects an acute process such as an infection, so it should be repeated once you are well -- about two weeks later. The tempting wrong answer is concluding high cardiovascular risk: the AHA/CDC risk bands (under 1, 1-3, above 3) only apply to a stable baseline value. CRP is also nonspecific, so it is a clue for your doctor to interpret alongside other risk factors, not a diagnosis.",
